<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Umbria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Umbria is a landlocked region of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> valley. It takes its name from the ancient Umbrians, and its hill towns, among them Perugia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assisi">Assisi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> and Orvieto, were long part of the Papal States before joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1860. Assisi made it the homeland of Saint Francis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/umbria.jpg" alt="Umbria" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Umbria_in_Italy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Umbria is the only Italian region with neither a coastline nor a border with another country. It lies between Tuscany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>, includes part of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a>, and is crossed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a>. Its capital is Perugia. In antiquity a pair of shallow lakes, the Lacus Clitorius and the Lacus Umber, covered the plain; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> drained them, an earthquake in the 4th century let the basin refill, and Benedictine monks began draining it again in the 13th century, a work completed in the 18th.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region is named for the Umbri, an Italic people who spoke Umbrian, a language related to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscan-language">Oscan</a>. Unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a>, they mostly lived in small dwellings in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a>. The Etruscans, their chief enemies, drove them towards the uplands from about 700 to 500 BCE, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> as the border. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iguvine-tablets">Iguvine Tablets</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gubbio">Gubbio</a>, written in Umbrian around the turn of the 2nd and 1st centuries BCE, are the longest and most important document of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osco-umbrian-languages">Osco-Umbrian languages</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> defeated the Samnites and their Gallic allies at Sentinum in 295 BCE, which began a period of integration; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> founded colonies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoletium</a> and built the Via Flaminia in 219 BCE. Perugia took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>'s side in 40 BCE and was almost completely destroyed by Octavian. After Rome's fall, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> fought over the region, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> founded the Duchy of Spoleto.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> kingdoms, some Umbrian territories passed to the Pope. Cities gained autonomy as comuni, and families such as the Baglioni in Perugia held power as signori. By the middle of the 14th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gil-alvarez-carrillo-de-albornoz">Cardinal Albornoz</a> had brought the region into the Papal States, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> ruled it uncontested until the end of the 18th century. Umbria was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-french-empire">Napoleonic Empire</a> from 1809 to 1814 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Trasimène. In 1860 the people of Perugia destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rocca-paolina">Rocca Paolina</a>, the fortress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iii">Pope Paul III</a> had built there, in the year the region was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Umbria is divided into the provinces of Perugia and Terni. Assisi, associated with Saint Francis, is a World Heritage Site. Gubbio holds the Festival of the Ceri each 15 May, a race held every year since 1160, and the Ceri appear on the region's coat of arms. Umbria is also where the umber pigment was originally extracted.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Umbria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>That May Giuseppe Garibaldi’s Expedition of the Thousand landed in Sicily and overthrew the Bourbons in the south, and Sardinian troops marched through the Papal Marche and <b>Umbria</b> to meet him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Roman Campagna was given over to great estates and grazing, increasingly malarial and thinly populated, while the Marche and <b>Umbria</b> grew grain, wine and olives, and Bologna, the richest city of the state, had a large silk industry and Europe’s oldest university.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Arretium, Etruscan Aritim, modern Arezzo, lay where the Val di Chiana meets the upper Arno, controlling routes between Etruria, <b>Umbria</b> and the Po valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/arretium">Arretium</a></li><li><blockquote>Though within the lands claimed by the Papal States, it governed itself and conquered much of central <b>Umbria</b>, including rival Assisi and Foligno.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-perugia">Commune of Perugia</a></li><li><blockquote>The duchy grew into a large territory covering much of <b>Umbria</b>, the Marche and Abruzzo, largely independent of the kings in Pavia.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-spoleto">Duchy of Spoleto</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1395 Gian Galeazzo bought the title of duke from Emperor Wenceslas, turning the lordship into the Duchy of Milan, which he expanded towards Tuscany and <b>Umbria</b> before his death in 1402.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-milan">Lordship of Milan</a></li></ul></section>
